# Clean Architecture & Python Conventions

This project follows **pure Clean Architecture**. Respect layer boundaries and never skip them.

## Layer structure

```
app/
├── domain/           # Business rules — no framework imports
│   ├── entities/     # Domain entities (plain Python classes)
│   ├── repositories/ # Abstract repository contracts (*_repository_abstract.py)
│   ├── use_cases/    # Base use case orchestration
│   ├── steps/        # Base step contract
│   ├── auth/         # Role → permission mapping (pure Python)
│   └── enums/        # RoleEnum, PermissionEnum
├── application/      # Application logic
│   ├── use_cases/    # One use case per action (*_use_case.py)
│   ├── steps/        # One step per use case operation (*_step.py)
│   └── dtos/         # Internal data transfer objects (*_dto.py)
├── adapters/         # External implementations
│   ├── repositories/ # Concrete repositories (*_repository.py)
│   ├── models/       # SQLAlchemy ORM models
│   └── container.py  # Dependency injection wiring
├── core/             # Cross-cutting infrastructure
│   ├── auth/deps.py  # Permission dependency aliases
│   ├── dependencies.py
│   ├── security.py   # JWT
│   └── cache_utils/  # Redis session cache
└── presentation/     # HTTP layer
    └── api/v1/
        ├── endpoints/  # Controllers (call use cases only)
        └── schemas/      # Pydantic request/response models
```

## Dependency rule

Dependencies point **inward only**:

- `presentation` → `application` → `domain`
- `adapters` → `domain` (implements domain contracts)
- `domain` must **never** import from `application`, `adapters`, or `presentation`

## Request flow

```
Endpoint → UseCase.run() → Step._run() → Repository → ORM
```

- **Endpoints** map Pydantic schemas to DTOs and call `use_case.run()`. No business logic.
- **Use cases** are `@dataclass` classes extending `BaseUseCase`, wiring steps in `__post_init__`.
- **Steps** extend `BaseUseCaseStep`, implement `_run()`, and call `self.use_case.repository`.
- **Repositories** hold all persistence methods. Never put DB logic in use cases, steps, or endpoints.

## Repository naming

| Layer | Class | File |
|-------|-------|------|
| Domain contract | `{Entity}RepositoryAbstract` | `domain/repositories/{entity}_repository_abstract.py` |
| Adapter impl | `{Entity}Repository` | `adapters/repositories/{entity}_repository.py` |

- Always add new persistence methods to the **abstract contract first**, then implement in the concrete `Repository` class.
- Inject repositories via `UserRepositoryAbstract` (domain), never the concrete class outside `adapters/`.

## Naming conventions (Pythonic)

| Element | Style | Example |
|---------|-------|---------|
| Modules / files | `snake_case` | `create_user_use_case.py`, `cattle_data.py` |
| Functions / methods | `snake_case` | `get_user_by_email`, `create_cattle` |
| Variables / attributes | `snake_case` | `company_id`, `full_name` |
| Classes | `PascalCase` | `User`, `CreateUserUseCase`, `UserRepository` |
| Constants | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_RETRIES` |
| Entity files | `snake_case` | `domain/entities/cattle_data.py` |
| Entity classes | `PascalCase` | `class CattleData:` |
| Use cases | `{Action}{Entity}UseCase` | `CreateUserUseCase` |
| Steps | `{Action}{Entity}Step` | `CreateUserStep` |
| DTOs | `{Entity}{Action}DTO` | `UserCreateDTO`, `UserResponseDTO` |

## Adding a new feature checklist

1. Entity in `domain/entities/{entity}.py` (if needed)
2. Abstract methods in `domain/repositories/{entity}_repository_abstract.py`
3. Implementation in `adapters/repositories/{entity}_repository.py`
4. DTOs in `application/dtos/{entity}_dto.py`
5. Step in `application/steps/{entity}/{action}_{entity}_step.py`
6. Use case in `application/use_cases/{entity}/{action}_{entity}_use_case.py`
7. Pydantic schema in `presentation/api/v1/schemas/{entity}.py`
8. Endpoint in `presentation/api/v1/endpoints/{entity}.py` (use permission aliases from `core/auth/deps.py`)
9. Wire in `adapters/container.py` and `bootstrap/routes.py`
10. If new action: add `PermissionEnum` + update `ROLE_PERMISSIONS` (see `auth-permissions` rule)

## Authentication & permissions

See **`auth-permissions.mdc`** for the full pattern. Summary:

- Endpoints use `CanCreateCattleDep`, `CompanyIdDep`, etc. — never `require_roles(...)`
- Permission logic lives in `domain/auth/role_permissions.py`
- JWT + Redis session cache — no DB lookup per request

## Do not

- Put business logic in endpoints, repositories, or ORM models
- Import SQLAlchemy, FastAPI, or Pydantic inside `domain/`
- Call repository methods directly from endpoints
- Create use cases without steps (except orchestration base classes)
- Use Portuguese or non-English identifiers (see `english-only` rule)
- List roles in endpoints — use permission aliases (see `auth-permissions` rule)
